-- title:
--   Cartier module modulo p is free of rank h
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $k$ be a perfect field of characteristic $p$ (perfectness being the assumption that the $p$-th power map is bijective). Let $\Phi$ be a $d$-dimensional formal group law over $k$: a $d$-tuple of power series in the variables indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,d \oplus \mathrm{Fin}\,d$ with vanishing constant terms, with linear coefficients $\delta_{ij}$ in each of the two groups of variables, and satisfying the associativity identity under substitution; assume moreover that $\Phi$ is commutative, i.e. swapping the two groups of variables fixes each component. Let $h$ be a natural number and assume that the $k$-vector space $k[[X_1,\dots,X_d]]/I$, where $I$ is generated by the components of `Φ.nthSeries p` (the $p$-fold iterate defined by $0$ at stage $0$ and by substituting the previous stage together with the identity into $\Phi$, i.e. the $[p]$-series of $\Phi$), has finite rank exactly $p^h$. Let $M$ denote [`MvFormalGroup.CartierModule p Φ`](def/MvFormalGroup_CartierModule.html#L162), the module of $d$-tuples of power series in variables indexed by $\mathbb{N}$ with vanishing constant terms which transform the Witt addition law into addition along $\Phi$, with its $W(k)$-module structure. Then there exists a basis indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,h$ of $M/\big((p)\cdot M\big)$ as a module over $W(k)/(p)$, where $(p)$ is the ideal of $W(k)$ generated by $p$; that is, $M/pM$ is free of rank $h$ over $W(k)/(p)$.
--
--   This is the module-theoretic reading of the height of a formal group law: the height $h$, defined through the order $p^h$ of the kernel of multiplication by $p$, is the rank of $M/pM$ for the Cartier module $M$ of $\Phi$. It feeds the construction of a $W(k)$-basis of $M$ itself ([`MvFormalGroup.CartierModule.nonempty_basis_of_finrank_eq_pow`](thm.html#MvFormalGroup.CartierModule.nonempty_basis_of_finrank_eq_pow)) and the rank computation for the formal module attached to a maximal order in a quaternion algebra used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd setting.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvFormalGroup_CartierModule_nonempty_basis_quotient_smul_top_of_finrank_eq_pow.lean

